# Sensor Replacement and Adapter Layer
This is a very useful example because it connects System Architecture directly with Software Architecture.
Assume the original system uses: Radar A
The software directly communicates with Radar A: Obstacle Detection -> Radar A API -> Radar A
Later, the system engineers decide to replace Radar A with Radar B.
But Radar B has a different API:
Radar A: getDistance()
Radar B: readObjectData()
If the application knows Radar A's API directly, replacing the sensor may require changes throughout the software.
This creates strong coupling: Application -> Specific Radar
Adapter Layer : Instead, we can introduce an abstraction:
            Obstacle Detection
                  ↓
            Radar Interface
                  ↓
            Adapter Layer
            ↙       ↘
Radar A Adapter   Radar B Adapter
          ↓           ↓
      Radar A       Radar B

The application works with a common interface, for example:
Radar Interface
getObjects()
getDistance()
getStatus()
Each adapter translates between the common interface and the specific radar implementation.

Why?
Because we may have an important quality attribute:
Modifiability
We want to be able to replace the sensor without changing large parts of the software.
So the reasoning becomes:
System-Level Need
↓
Different sensors may need to be supported
 
System Architecture Decision
↓
Sensor technology must be replaceable
 
Software Quality Attribute
↓
Modifiability
 
Software Architecture Decision
↓
Use an abstraction / adapter layer
